HEVC frame preparation and Apple payload development
===================================================

The HEVC frame_ops callbacks are CPU-only. prepare() consumes the controls
already applied by the core, snapshots all fixed and dynamic controls under the
handler lock, writes a bounded little-endian instruction program, and retains
its private state until retire(). Every global frame index has an independent
scratch-buffer set. dep is NONE. The core must drain before stop().

The legacy core bridge applies/completes controls and translates the same
program back to avd_job segments. Its old PP kick lives in avd-drv.c, not in
prepare(). This retains the existing single-job path until rewrite-core replaces
that dispatch. It is not evidence of queued firmware execution.

Slice layout selection checks two complete descriptions:

* Per-NAL bit_size values tile bytesused minus the VB2 plane data_offset. The
  entropy range begins at the NAL prefix sum plus data_byte_offset.
* Absolute data_byte_offset values delimit disjoint entropy ranges; bit_size
  describes entropy data only. Gaps for NAL headers are allowed.

Invalid descriptions, overflow, overlapping absolute ranges and ambiguous
unequal ranges fail before instruction generation. No bitstream copy or CPU
mapping of OUTPUT is needed. Both modes use the same tile/entry-point builder.

Apple readiness (deliberately incomplete)
----------------------------------------

avd-hevc-fp.c records the licensed V3 offsets from:
https://github.com/eiln/avd/blob/main/avid/h265/fp.py
https://github.com/eiln/avd/blob/main/avid/h265/halv3.py
https://github.com/eiln/avd/blob/main/avid/h265/decoder.py
MIT, Copyright 2023 Eileen Yoon <eyn@gmx.com>.

The writer mirrors known header/scaling/reference/first-slice instruction
fields into the V3 slot. Array capacities are bounded by the public structure.
It does not invent a second-slice stride, input-descriptor layout for multiple
slices, or PIO control words. Main10 candidate offsets remain table entries;
Linux instruction bit-depth bits are not copied into the Apple slot as facts.

The Borage table records the 0x1130d0 slot and section 6.3 command constants.
The common frame number and slice count can be written. Its syntax, work-array
assignment, and packed cmd+0x3c/+0x44 address masks remain unresolved. The command
helper validates four available count fields against active_vps (1..4), the
slice count (<0x258), monotonically increasing aligned in-slot work offsets and
per-VP work <=0x1000/active_vps. The actual firmware traversal count and the work
unit/extent are still unproved. pack_cmd() never succeeds for an incomplete
Apple payload; known command-field tests do not establish firmware acceptance.

T8112 and T8140 have explicit TODO rows tied to the per-SoC ledger task t-3862.
T8103 selects the public M1 V3 comparison layout. T6000 still needs the
ledger's runtime class confirmation and stays UNKNOWN. T6040 selects Borage. Other matches stay UNKNOWN,
without falling back to a different generation. HEVC capability and Apple
payload readiness are separate. All existing HEVC-capable matches advertise
S265/Main/Main10, including T6040; Main10 output is the existing P010 format.

Host checks (run in an approved buildbox slot)
--------------------------------------------

python3 drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/tests/test-hevc-program.py \
  --baseline-ref fbc4e7934ca7325f3483b72a51d24c74aa8536a4

cc -std=gnu11 -O1 -g -Wall -Wextra -Werror -fsanitize=address,undefined \
  -DAVD_HEVC_HOST_TEST -Idrivers/media/platform/apple/avd \
  drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/tests/hevc-fp-test.c \
  drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-hevc-fp.c \
  drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-hevc-slices.c -o /tmp/hevc-fp-test
/tmp/hevc-fp-test

The first test compiles the actual old/new builder bodies with a minimal host
shim. It compares every instruction word for 96 combinations of bit depth 8/10,
CTU 32/64, tiles 1x1/2x2, I/P/B and four instruction-header/address generations,
with both slice layouts (192 comparisons). These are synthetic V4L2 controls;
they are not controls captured from libx265 or an output-pixel decode test.
The second exercises normalization failures, V3 field offsets/capacities,
Main10 candidate rejection and Borage command/work validation boundaries.
